-- WITH holds queries only: no DML inside it.
-- Archive, then delete, as two statements:
INSERT INTO jobs_archive
SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE status = 'done';

DELETE FROM jobs WHERE status = 'done';
COMMIT;
-- each statement takes its own snapshot: a job
-- that turns 'done' in between is deleted
-- without being archived. Close the gap with a
-- lock, or PL/SQL DELETE ... RETURNING BULK COLLECT.

deeper.body:
A CTE that changes data is worth trying once, inside a transaction you then undo. A transaction groups statements so that they all take effect together or not at all: `BEGIN` starts one, and `ROLLBACK` throws all its changes away. This one moves the finished jobs to an archive table in one statement:

```sql
BEGIN;
CREATE TEMP TABLE jobs_archive (LIKE jobs);

WITH moved AS (
    DELETE FROM jobs
    WHERE status = 'done'
    RETURNING *
)
INSERT INTO jobs_archive
SELECT * FROM moved;

SELECT job_id, kind, status FROM jobs_archive ORDER BY job_id;
SELECT count(*) AS left_in_jobs FROM jobs;
ROLLBACK;
```

The INSERT reports `INSERT 0 2`. The archive holds jobs 1 (`fraud_review`) and 4 (`send_receipt`), both `done`, and 6 jobs are left in `jobs`. ROLLBACK then puts everything back.

All parts of the statement see the same snapshot of the data: a fixed picture taken when the statement starts. That has a surprising result. The main query can't see the changes its own CTEs make to tables. `WITH d AS (DELETE FROM jobs WHERE status = 'done') SELECT count(*) FROM jobs` still counts 8, because the SELECT reads the picture from before the DELETE. Only the RETURNING rows carry the changes forward. The PostgreSQL documentation also warns against changing the same row twice in one statement: which change wins can't be predicted.

Subqueries have one more shape worth knowing by name. A **row subquery** returns one row with several columns and compares them all at once. `WHERE (card_id, amount_minor) = (SELECT card_id, amount_minor FROM payments WHERE payment_id = 17)` finds payments with the same card and amount as payment 17: here, 17 and its duplicate 18. "LATERAL joins, top-N per group and keyset pagination" uses the same row comparison, with `<`, to page through results.

Finally, `ANY` and `ALL` with an empty list. `x > ALL (empty)` is true, because there's no element it fails against. `x > (SELECT max(...) FROM empty)` compares with NULL, so it's never true. The two look interchangeable, and they part ways exactly when the subquery finds nothing.
